*Title: Hacking Guide to Lyntin
*Author: Will


* VERSION

$Id: HACKING,v 1.5 2002/03/25 02:11:10 willhelm Exp $

For more up-to-date information, please check the web-site:
http://lyntin.sourceforge.net/



* NOTES

This entire document is written off the top of my head and the contents
of it are likely to change before 3.0 actually gets released.  It's
possible this document will at some point get merged with the ARCHITECTURE
document.



* SYNOPSIS

Lyntin was built for the hacker/mudder.  This is a special breed of
person who is mudding and has a series of complex problems they want
to solve and mere aliases and triggers just aren't going to do the
trick.

Tintin++ has a series of commands that almost but not quite implements
(ed. bad word choice) a programming language.  Building "macros" in 
this way is complex and solves a small set of problems, but the more 
complex problems really need a full language and a library to solve.  
As such, Lyntin was built with Python and allows hackers to use Python 
to enhance Lyntin.

Because Lyntin is so inextricably intertwined with Python, you can
extend Lyntin to do anything without going through hoops: check your
email, record URLs you see and open up a browser to visit them, export
mudmail, building complex bots....

There a a few different methods of hacking Lyntin to suit your needs.  
They are briefly outlined here and described in more detail in their 
respective sections:

	1. editing the source code
	2. building modules
	3. command line

Before we continue, we should cover Lyntin's architecture first.



* DIRECTORIES

^<<
   lyntinng/
      |-- ui/            -- user interfaces
      |-- modules/       -- Lyntin modules (dynamically loaded at runtime)
      |-- help/          -- Lyntin help files
      |-- errata/        -- random stuff for testing and other stuff
^>>



* ARCHITECTURE

^<<
engine.Engine
   |-- 1 help.HelpManager
   |-- * session.Session
   |      |-- 1 net.SocketCommunicator
   |      |-- 1 alias.AliasManager           (inherited from common)
   |      |-- 1 action.ActionManager         (inherited from common)
   |      |-- 1 gag.GagManager               (inherited from common)
   |      |-- 1 highlight.HighlightManager   (inherited from common)
   |      |-- 1 substitute.SubstituteManager (inherited from common)
   |      |-- 1 variable.VariableManager     (inherited from common)
   |      |-- 1 ticker.Ticker                (not inherited from common)
   |
   |-- 1 history.HistoryManager
   |-- 1 threadmanager.ThreadManager
   |-- 1 ui.Baseui subclass
^>>

The engine holds the event queue and drives Lyntin.  Most Lyntin
things occur via an event.  Lyntin starts with the StartupEvent and ends
with the ShutdownEvent.  The event queue is a synchronized queue and
thus gives us a centralized way of synchronizing all the threads
accessing Lyntin internals without causing them to block.

The engine holds:

	* the HistoryManager
	* the ThreadManager
	* the HelpManager
	* the list of Sessions
	* the user interface

All these things exist on the 'engine scoping'.

Each session has:

	* its own SocketCommunicator which abstracts socket
	  connections and network issues
	* a series of managers which manage aliases, actions,
	  gags, highlights, substitutes, variables and other such 
	  things
	* a Ticker which allows you to do things at intervals

These things exist on a 'session scoping' in the sense that there
exists one of each of these for every session.



* MANAGERS

Managers manage things.  All the session-scoped managers subclass
the 'manager.Manager' class.  It doesn't really provide a lot of
functionality, but it allows us to group them all and treat them 
all the same.  Adding new managers is much easier because of this.



* DATA HANDLING

Data comes from one of a couple of sources: the user or the mud.
User data (sometimes called Input) is received from the ui.BaseUI
subclasses and tossed into an 'event.InputEvent'.

Data can also come from the mud.  Mud data comes from the 
SocketCommunicator and is encapsulated in an 'event.MudEvent'.



* UI

All user interfaces extend the ui.BaseUI class in the ui module.
It has a series of methods which need to be overridden to give
the ui functionality.  In addition, ui's can specify additional
Lyntin commands, they can bind to Lyntin hooks (most ui's bind
to the STARTUPHOOK allowing them to perform certain functionality
on Lyntin startup rather than module loading time), and anything
else that modules can do.

Lyntin comes with a text ui which can be used over telnet/ssh
or in a command shell.  It also comes with a tk gui.  The tk gui
has functionality like split history screen (pgup and pgdown),
its own command history buffer (up and down) and numpad bindings
(VK__NUMPAD0 through VK__NUMPAD9).  There's also a very rudimentary
and unstable curses ui.

Feel free to extend the ui code and send in patches.  Or even
write your own ui based on another widget set or whatever you
want to do.



* HOOKS

The engine is augmented by a series of hooks which allow modules to
execute functions without having to change Lyntin's internals.  An
example of this would be the startup and shutdown hooks.  Any functions
that hook into these hooks will be executed upon startup or shutdown
of Lyntin.  Lyntin also uses these hooks to implement some of its
functionality.

For example, the TkGui uses the STARTUPHOOK to instantiate its gui 
interface and set itself up.



* SOURCE CODE HACKING

Lyntin is entirely written in Python.  The source code is well organized
(as of 3.0) and documentation exists on the web-site as well as in the 
source code distribution on how Lyntin is architected and details on
the internals.

Lyntin is distributed under the GPL.  You should read this prior to
making changes to the source code and distributing them so you don't
accidentally find yourself in violation.

If you patch Lyntin, we'd love to see your work!  Send it to the
Lyntin developers list and depending on what the patch does, it may
get incorporated into the Lyntin base code.



* LYNTIN MODULE HACKING

Lyntin is written in Python.  Each file that ends in a .py is a
Python module.  Lyntin has modules as well.  A Lyntin module is
a Python module found in the modules/ subdirectory which has a 
'load' method.

Lyntin modules are cool.  All of Lyntin's commands are implemented 
in the 'basic', 'advanced', and 'unittest' Lyntin modules.  Because
Lyntin modules are written in Python, they have full access to
the full Python API--they're not run in any kind of rexec bastion
or anything.

Lyntin modules use the 'exported' module to access Lyntin internals.
This API is guaranteed not to change (much) over versions of Lyntin.

All modules are loaded dynamically when Lyntin starts.  The code
for this is in modules/____init____.py.  You don't have to register your
module, but you do have to implement a 'load' function which gets
called at startup.  This is where your module would bind to hooks,
add Lyntin commands, and do whatever other startup things you need.

Since Lyntin's commands are implemented in Lyntin modules, you can
use them as an example for writing your own.



* COMMAND LINE HACKING

Lyntin allows you to execute arbitrary Python at the command line.
The code will be executed in some module--(to be determined).

For example:

^<<
	#@print "hello"
^>>

will print hello to the console.  There are some significant differences
between Lyntin 2.x and 3.x in how this works.  In the 2.x series, 
raw Python was executed in the user.py module.  It was encouraged that
you add your own functions to the user.py module and then these would
be available to you at the Lyntin command line.

In 3.0 user.py is a module in the modules directory.  It does not come
with Lyntin--it's something you'll have to create.  This is a little
weird in that you have to go write your own user.py module, but on the
flip side, it helps when you upgrade Lyntin--you won't accidentally
overwrite your user.py Lyntin module because it doesn't come with
Lyntin.

If you don't have a user.py module, raw Python code gets executed in 
the advanced.py module where the #@ functionality exists.

A basic user.py module would be as follows:

^<<
"""
This is my user.py module.  I can do whatever I want in here.
"""
import exported

myvar = "hello"

def myfunc():
  exported.write_message(myvar)


def load():
  """
  This is required for this module to dynamically load.
  You can put startup bindings in this function.  Look
  at the other Lyntin modules for examples.
  """
  pass
^>>

Now at the Lyntin command prompt I can do things like:

^<<
> #@print myvar
hello
> #@myfunc()
lyntin: hello
^>>



* END

That about covers it.  If you have questions, sign onto the Lyntin
developers list and ask them.  The list is low volume high signal.

	http://sourceforge.net/mail/?group_id=6730

Happy hacking!

